@*
    Why the import stops at every note. Without this the walk reads as needless friction —
    extract, review, extract, review — and the natural reaction is to look for the button that
    turns it off. The pacing IS the feature, so it has to say so where the waiting happens.

    Collapsed by default: a reason you have already read becomes noise by note twelve.
*@

<div class="nornis-pacing-note">
    <MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Outlined.Lightbulb" Size="Size.Small" Color="Color.Secondary" />
    <span class="nornis-pacing-lead">@Lead</span>
    <MudButton Variant="Variant.Text" Size="Size.Small" Class="nornis-pacing-toggle"
               OnClick="() => _open = !_open">
        @(_open ? "Hide" : "Why?")
    </MudButton>
</div>

@if (_open)
{
    <div class="nornis-pacing-detail">
        <p>
            Read all at once, every note would be read against the same empty record — and each
            would introduce its own copy of the same people and places. Three notes mentioning
            the same captain would propose three captains, and your review would be spent merging
            duplicates instead of checking whether the facts are right.
        </p>
        <p>
            Going one at a time, each note is read against the record your earlier decisions
            built. The second note already knows who the first introduced, so it adds to them
            rather than inventing them again — and what you accept along the way is what the
            next note gets to build on.
        </p>
        <p class="nornis-pacing-aside">
            You can skip a note at any point, and stop the import whenever you like. Notes you
            have already reviewed keep everything they contributed.
        </p>
    </div>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The always-visible sentence. Differs before the walk starts and during it.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string Lead { get; set; } = "Nornis reads one note at a time, on purpose.";

    private bool _open;
}
